<exercises label="uc-exercises">

	<exercisegroup xml:id="uc-concept-quiz">
		<title>💡 Conceptual Quiz</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				Answer the following questions to test your understanding of the method of undetermined coefficients.
			</p>
			<aside>
				<title>Abbreviations</title>
				<tabular>
					<col width="10%" />
					<col width="90%" />
					<row>
						<cell><p><em>DE</em></p></cell>
						<cell><em>Differential Equation</em></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><p><em>IVP</em></p></cell>
						<cell><em>Initial Value Problem</em></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><p><em>LHCC</em></p></cell>
						<cell><em>Linear Homogeneous Constant Coefficients</em></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><p><em>LNCC</em></p></cell>
						<cell><em>Linear Nonhomogeneous Constant Coefficients</em></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</aside>
		</introduction>

		<exercise label="uc-cq">

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-08">
				<title>Modifying the Initial Guess</title>

				<statement>
					<p>When solving <m>y'' - 2y' + y = e^x</m>, the initial guess <m>Ae^x</m> for <m>y_p</m> must be multiplied by <m>x</m> twice, becoming <m>Ax^2 e^x</m>, because of repeated overlap with the homogeneous solution <m>y_h =</m> <fillin characters="10" />.</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><m>c_1 e^x + c_2 x e^x</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct! The characteristic equation <m>(r-1)^2 = 0</m> has the double root <m>r = 1</m>. The guess <m>Ae^x</m> overlaps with <m>c_1 e^x</m>, so it becomes <m>Axe^x</m>; that overlaps with <m>c_2 x e^x</m>, so it becomes <m>Ax^2 e^x</m>.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>c_1 e^x + c_2 e^{-x}</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. The characteristic equation is <m>(r-1)^2 = 0</m>, so <m>r = -1</m> is not a root. With this <m>y_h</m>, the guess would need only one factor of <m>x</m>.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>c_1 e^x + c_2 x^2 e^x</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. The double root <m>r = 1</m> gives <m>e^x</m> and <m>xe^x</m>, not <m>x^2 e^x</m>. If <m>x^2 e^x</m> were in <m>y_h</m>, the guess <m>Ax^2 e^x</m> would still overlap.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>c_1 \cos(x) + c_2 \sin(x)</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. The roots of <m>(r-1)^2 = 0</m> are real, so <m>y_h</m> has no sine or cosine terms.</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-01">
				<title>Purpose of the Method</title>

				<statement>
					<p>What is the main purpose of the Method of Undetermined Coefficients?</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p>To solve the homogeneous solution <m>y_h</m> directly.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>To determine the coefficients in the particular solution <m>y_p</m>.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p>To find the characteristic equation roots.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p>To verify initial conditions are satisfied.</p></statement></choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-02">
				<title>When It Works</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						The Method of Undetermined Coefficients applies when the forcing function is:
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A polynomial function.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>An exponential function.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A sine or cosine function.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p>A logarithmic function.</p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p>A tangent or cotangent function.</p></statement></choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-03">
				<title>Choose <m>y_p</m></title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose <md>y'' - 4y' + 3y = 6e^x</md>. Which of the following is the correct selection for <m>y_p</m>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="no">
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = Ae^x</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. Remember to consider whether <m>e^x</m> overlaps with any terms in <m>y_h</m>.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = Axe^x</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct! Since <m>e^x</m> is a term in <m>y_h</m>, we need to multiply the particular solution by <m>x</m> to ensure independence.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="no">
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = Ax + B</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. This form is better suited to a polynomial right-hand side, such as <m>f(x) = 6x</m>.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="no">
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = A</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. A constant form of <m>y_p</m> would only be appropriate if <m>f(x)</m> were a constant.</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-04">
				<title>Selecting the particular form</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Select the initial form of the particular solution for an LNCC equation with forcing function:
						<md>
							f(x) = 5x^2 + 3x
						</md>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = Ax^2 + Bx + C</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct! Since the forcing function is a 2nd degree polynomial, the particular solution should be the most general 2nd degree polynomial.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = A e^{x} + B</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. The forcing function does not contain an exponential function, so neither should the particular solution.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = A x^2 + B x</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. This form is missing a free term.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>\ds\quad y_p = Ax^3 + Bx^2 + Cx + D</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect. The initial form matches the degree of <m>f(x)</m>, so the <m>x^3</m> term does not belong. Higher powers of <m>x</m> enter <m>y_p</m> only through the modification step: if the form <m>Ax^2 + Bx + C</m> shares a term with <m>y_h</m>, the whole form is multiplied by <m>x</m>, and again if the new form still shares a term. Padding the guess with <m>x^3</m> does not always work: for <m>y'' = 5x^2 + 3x</m> no choice of <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>D</m> works, because the particular solution needs an <m>x^4</m> term.</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-05">
				<title>Select the correct form</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the most appropriate form for the particular solution to
						<md>
							y'' - 4y' + 3y = 9x + 6
						</md>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>\ds\quad Ax + B</m>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! The forcing function is a first-degree polynomial, so the particular solution should generalize this form as <m>Ax + B</m>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>\ds\quad Ae^{9x}</m>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The forcing function is a polynomial, not an exponential function.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>\ds\quad A\sin(9x)</m>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The forcing function is a polynomial, not a trigonometric function.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>\ds\quad A</m>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The forcing function is not a constant.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-06">
				<title>Classifying Practice</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Select each classification label that applies to the equation
						<md>y'' = y' + 6y</md>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Linear</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, each of the terms are linear.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Homogeneous</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, the free term is zero.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Constant Coefficients</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, each coefficient is constant.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>LHCC</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct!</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-07">
				<title>Classifying Practice</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Select each classification label that applies to the equation
						<md>3y''' + y'- \sin(y) = 0</md>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement><p>Linear</p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect, <m>\sin(y)</m> is a nonlinear term.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>Homogeneous</p></statement>
						<feedback>Technically, only linear equations can be labeled as homogeneous or not. Since the equation is nonlinear, we do not select it. </feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>Constant Coefficients</p></statement>
						<feedback>Technically, only linear equations can be labeled as having constant coefficients or not. Since the equation is nonlinear, we do not select it. </feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>LHCC</p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect.</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-09">
				<title>Classifying Practice</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Select each classification label that applies to the equation
						<md>y''- 6 = 0</md>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Linear</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, both terms are linear.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>Homogeneous</p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect, the free term, <m>6</m>, is non-zero. </feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Constant Coefficients</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, each coefficient is constant. </feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>LHCC</p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect.</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-tf-10">
				<title>Classifying Practice</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Select each classification label that applies to the equation
						<md>\frac{d^3y}{dt^3} + k\frac{dy}{dt} = ty, \qquad k \text{ is constant}</md>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Linear</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, all terms are linear.</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>Homogeneous</p></statement>
						<feedback>Correct, the free term is zero. </feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>Constant Coefficients</p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect, the <m>y</m> term coefficient, <m>t</m>, is not constant. </feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>LHCC</p></statement>
						<feedback>Incorrect.</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-sa-01">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Suppose you knew that the final form of the particular solution of an LNCC equation was
						<m>y_p = Axe^x</m>.
						Using only this information, list out everything you can deduce about the differential equation.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<response />
				<answer>
					<p>
						The starting form must have been <m>Ae^x</m>, so the forcing function is a nonzero multiple of <m>e^x</m>: <m>f(x) = ke^x</m> with <m>k \neq 0</m>. The form was multiplied by <m>x</m> exactly once, so <m>e^x</m> appears in <m>y_h</m> but <m>xe^x</m> does not. That means <m>r = 1</m> is a root of the characteristic equation with multiplicity exactly one. For a second-order equation, the other root <m>r_2</m> is real (complex roots come in conjugate pairs) and <m>r_2 \neq 1</m>, so the characteristic equation is <m>a(r-1)(r-r_2) = 0</m> and <m>y_h = c_1 e^x + c_2 e^{r_2 x}</m>. After dividing by the leading coefficient, the equation has the form <m>y'' - (1+r_2)y' + r_2 y = ke^x</m>.
					</p>
				</answer>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-sa-02">
				<statement>
					<p>When solving LNCC equations, why do we multiply a form in the guess for <m>y_p</m> by <m>x</m> when one of its terms also appears in the homogeneous solution <m>y_h</m>?</p>
				</statement>
				<response />
				<answer>
					<p>
						Every term of <m>y_h</m> solves the homogeneous equation, so substituting it into the left-hand side gives <m>0</m>. A term of the guess that also appears in <m>y_h</m> therefore contributes nothing toward <m>f(x)</m>, and the coefficient equations have no solution. Multiplying every term of the form that contains the shared term by <m>x</m> (repeating if needed) produces terms that are not in <m>y_h</m>. Those terms survive substitution and can be matched to <m>f(x)</m>. Only that form is multiplied; the other forms in <m>y_p</m> are left alone. For example, for <m>y'' - y = e^x + 1</m> the guess <m>Ae^x + B</m> becomes <m>Axe^x + B</m>, not <m>Axe^x + Bx</m>.
					</p>
				</answer>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-sa-03">
				<statement>
					<p>In the general solution <m>y = y_h + y_p</m> of an LNCC equation, what roles do <m>y_h</m> and <m>y_p</m> play, and why must the <em>guess</em> for <m>y_p</m> share no terms with <m>y_h</m>?</p>
				</statement>
				<response />
				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>y_h</m> is the general solution of the associated homogeneous equation (with <m>f(x)</m> replaced by <m>0</m>). It carries the arbitrary constants, which are later fixed by initial conditions, and it contributes <m>0</m> when substituted into the left-hand side. <m>y_p</m> is any one solution of the full equation; it is what produces the forcing function <m>f(x)</m>. A term of the guess that also appears in <m>y_h</m> is sent to <m>0</m> by the left-hand side, so its coefficient can never be matched to <m>f(x)</m>. That is why the guess is modified until it shares no terms with <m>y_h</m>. Once a particular solution is found, adding a multiple of a <m>y_h</m> term to it gives another particular solution, and the general solution stays the same.
					</p>
				</answer>
			</task>

			<task label="uc-cq-sa-04">
				<statement>
					<p>Describe the difference between a homogeneous and a non-homogeneous differential equation.</p>
				</statement>
				<response />
				<solution>
					<p>
						A differential equation is termed "homogeneous" if all the terms of the equation are either derivatives of the unknown function or multiples of the unknown function itself, and the equation can be set equal to zero. In other words, there are no terms in the equation that are solely functions of the independent variable or constants.
					</p>
					<p>					
						For example, the equation <m> y'' + 2y' + y = 0 </m> is homogeneous because all its terms involve <m> y </m> or its derivatives, and the equation is set equal to zero.
					</p>
					<p>					
						A differential equation is termed "non-homogeneous" if it contains terms that are solely functions of the independent variable or constants that are not multiples of the unknown function or its derivatives.
					</p>
					<p>
						For example, the equation <m> y'' + 2y' + y = e^x </m> is non-homogeneous because of the term <m> e^x </m> on the right side, which is not a derivative or multiple of <m> y </m>.
					</p>
					<p>
						The distinction between homogeneous and non-homogeneous differential equations is crucial, as the methods used to solve them can differ.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</exercisegroup>

	<exercisegroup cols="2">
		<title>🏋️‍♂️ Write Down the Form of <m>y_p</m></title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				For each forcing function <m>g(t)</m>, write down the correct <em>form</em> of the particular solution <m>y_p</m>.
				Don't solve for coefficients, just show the form of <m>y_p</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-02">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = e^{7t} + 6</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y_p = A e^{7t} + B</m></p></answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-03">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = 6t^2 - 7\sin(3t) + 9</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y_p = A t^2 + B t + C + D \cos(3t) + E \sin(3t)</m></p></answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-04">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = 10e^t - 5t e^{-8t} + 2 e^{-8t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Drop the redundant <m>e^{-8t}</m> guess that would duplicate another term. Final form:
					<m>y_p = A e^{t} + (B t + C) e^{-8t}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-05">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = t^2\cos t - 5t\sin t</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Only need the highest-degree polynomial guess:
					<m>y_p = (A t^2 + B t + C)\cos t + (D t^2 + E t + F)\sin t</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-06">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = 16e^{7t}\sin(10t)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Ignore the exponential, guess for <m>\sin(10t)</m>, then tack the exponential back on:
					<m>y_p = e^{7t}\left(A \cos(10t) + B \sin(10t)\right)</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-07">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = (9t^2 - 103t)\cos t</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Product of a quadratic polynomial and cosine. Write separate polynomials for sine and cosine:
					<m>y_p = (A t^2 + B t + C)\cos t + (D t^2 + E t + F)\sin t</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-08">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = - e^{-2t}(3 - 5t)\cos(9t)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Exponential times linear polynomial times cosine. Separate terms for sine and cosine:
					<m>y_p = e^{-2t}\left[(At + B)\cos(9t) + (Ct + D)\sin(9t)\right]</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-09">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = 4\cos(6t) - 9\sin(6t)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y_p = A \cos(6t) + B \sin(6t)</m></p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-10">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = - 2\sin t + \sin(14t) - 5\cos(14t)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y_p = A \cos t + B \sin t + C \cos(14t) + D \sin(14t)</m></p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-11">
			<statement><p><m>g(t) = 5e^{-3t} + e^{-3t}\cos(6t) - \sin(6t)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
			<p>
				Distinct terms (plain exponential, exponential times trig, and plain trig):
				<m>y_p = A e^{-3t} + e^{-3t}(B \cos(6t) + C \sin(6t)) + D \cos(6t) + E \sin(6t)</m>
			</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>		
	</exercisegroup>

	<exercisegroup>
		<title>🏋️‍♂️ Find the Initial Form of <m>y_p</m></title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				For each differential equation below, determine the correct <em>form</em> of the particular solution <m>y_p</m>. 
				<em>Do NOT solve for coefficients like <m>A, B, C</m>.</em>
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<!-- 1. Constant + trig (easiest) -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-12">
			<statement><p><m> 3z'' - 4z' - 12z = 17 + 2\cos(2\theta)</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m> z_p = A + B\cos(2\theta) + C\sin(2\theta) </m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First, solve the homogeneous equation <m>3z'' - 4z' - 12z = 0</m> to find <m>z_h</m>. 
					The characteristic equation is:
					<md>
						<mrow> 3r^2 - 4r - 12 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = \frac{2 \pm 2\sqrt{10}}{3} </mrow>
					</md>
					Since the roots are distinct and real, <m>z_h = c_1 e^{\frac{2 + 2\sqrt{10}}{3}\theta} + c_2 e^{\frac{2 - 2\sqrt{10}}{3}\theta}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The forcing term is <m>17 + 2\cos(2\theta)</m>, a constant plus a trig function. 
					Our initial guess is:
					<md>
						z_p = A + B\cos(2\theta) + C\sin(2\theta)
					</md>
					No duplicates with <m>z_h</m> occur, so this guess stands.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 2. Polynomial + trig -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-13">
			<statement><p><m> y'' - 3y' - 17y = x^2 + \cos x </m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m> y_p = (Ax^2 + Bx + C) + (D\cos x + E\sin x) </m></p></answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 3y' - 17y = 0</m>.
				<md>
					<mrow> r^2 - 3r - 17 \amp = 0 </mrow>
					<mrow> r \amp = \frac{3 \pm \sqrt{77}}{2} </mrow>
				</md>
				Distinct real roots give <m>y_h = c_1 e^{\frac{3 + \sqrt{77}}{2}x} + c_2 e^{\frac{3 - \sqrt{77}}{2}x}</m>.
			</p>
			<p>
				The forcing term is a 2nd-degree polynomial plus a cosine. Our guess is:
				<md>y_p = (Ax^2 + Bx + C) + (D\cos x + E\sin x)</md>
				None of these terms repeat <m>y_h</m>.
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 3. Exponential + trig -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-14">
			<statement><p><m> y'' + y' - 12y = 4e^{3t} + \cos(2t) </m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m> y_p = Ate^{3t} + B\cos(2t) + C\sin(2t) </m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>y'' + y' - 12y = 0</m>.
					<md>
						<mrow> r^2 + r - 12 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> (r+4)(r-3) \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = -4, 3 </mrow>
					</md>
					So, <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-4t} + c_2 e^{3t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The forcing term has <m>4e^{3t}</m> and <m>\cos(2t)</m>. Our first guess is:
					<md> y_p = Ae^{3t} + B\cos(2t) + C\sin(2t) </md>
					Since <m>e^{3t}</m> appears in <m>y_h</m>, we multiply that term by <m>t</m>:
					<md> y_p = Ate^{3t} + B\cos(2t) + C\sin(2t) </md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 4. Polynomial + exp-trig -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-15">
			<statement><p><m> y'' - 4y = 2t - e^{2t}\sin(3t) </m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m> y_p = (At + B) + e^{2t}\big(C\cos(3t) + D\sin(3t)\big) </m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>y'' - 4y = 0</m>.
					<md>
						<mrow> r^2 - 4 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = \pm 2 </mrow>
					</md>
					Distinct real roots give <m>y_h = c_1 e^{2t} + c_2 e^{-2t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The forcing term is <m>2t</m> (a first-degree polynomial) and <m>e^{2t}\sin(3t)</m>.
					Guess:
					<md> y_p = (At + B) + e^{2t}\big(C\cos(3t) + D\sin(3t)\big) </md>
					No repeats with <m>y_h</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 5. Exp + poly*exp -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-16">
			<statement><p><m> w'' - 4w' + 13w = e^{3x} + x^2e^{-x} </m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m> w_p = Ae^{3x} + (Bx^2 + Cx + D)e^{-x} </m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>w'' - 4w' + 13w = 0</m>.
					<md>
						<mrow> r^2 - 4r + 13 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = 2 \pm 3i </mrow>
					</md>
					Complex roots give <m>w_h = c_1 e^{2x}\cos(3x) + c_2 e^{2x}\sin(3x)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The forcing term has <m>e^{3x}</m> and <m>x^2e^{-x}</m>.
					Guess:
					<md> w_p = Ae^{3x} + (Bx^2 + Cx + D)e^{-x} </md>
					None of these terms appear in <m>w_h</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 6. Needs multi-step correction -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-17">
			<statement><p><m> y'' - 2y' + y = x^2 + e^x </m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m> y_p = (Ax^2 + Bx + C) + Dx^2 e^x </m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>y'' - 2y' + y = 0</m>.
					<md>
						<mrow> r^2 - 2r + 1 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = 1 \text{ (double root)} </mrow>
					</md>
					Double root gives <m>y_h = c_1 e^{x} + c_2 x e^{x}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Forcing term: <m>x^2 + e^x</m>.  
					First guess: <m>(Ax^2 + Bx + C) + De^x</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Since <m>e^x</m> appears in <m>y_h</m>, modify <m>De^x</m> → <m>Dxe^x</m>.  
					But <m>xe^x</m> is also in <m>y_h</m>, so we multiply again: <m>D x^2 e^x</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 7. Advanced combo requiring exp-trig correction -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-18">
			<statement><p><m> z'' - 4z' + 5z = te^{2t} + 2e^{2t}\sin t </m></p></statement>
			<answer>
			<p><m> z_p = (At + B)e^{2t} + e^{2t}(Ct\cos t + Dt\sin t) </m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>z'' - 4z' + 5z = 0</m>. The characteristic equation is:
					<md>
						<mrow> r^2 - 4r + 5 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = 2 \pm i </mrow>
					</md>
					Complex roots give <m>z_h = c_1 e^{2t}\cos t + c_2 e^{2t}\sin t</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Forcing term: <m>te^{2t} + 2e^{2t}\sin t</m>.  
					First guess: 
					<md> z_p = (At + B)e^{2t} + e^{2t}(C\cos t + D\sin t) </md>
					The <m>e^{2t}\cos t</m> and <m>e^{2t}\sin t</m> terms duplicate <m>z_h</m>, so we multiply that piece by <m>t</m>:
					<md> z_p = (At + B)e^{2t} + e^{2t}(Ct\cos t + Dt\sin t) </md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-19">
			<statement><p><m>7y'' - y' + 2y = x^3 \sin(4x) + x^2 \sin(4x)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m> y_p = (Ax^3 + Bx^2 + Cx + D)\cos(4x) + (Ex^3 + Fx^2 + Gx + H)\sin(4x) </m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Solve <m>7y'' - y' + 2y = 0</m>.
					<md>
						<mrow> 7r^2 - r + 2 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = \frac{1 \pm i\sqrt{55}}{14} </mrow>
					</md>
					Complex roots give <m>y_h = c_1 e^{x/14}\cos\left(\frac{\sqrt{55}}{14}x\right) + c_2 e^{x/14}\sin\left(\frac{\sqrt{55}}{14}x\right)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The forcing term is <m>(x^3 + x^2)\sin(4x)</m>, a cubic polynomial times <m>\sin(4x)</m>, so it is a single form.
					Use the highest power, <m>x^3</m>, and give the cosine and the sine each their own full cubic polynomial:
					<md> y_p = (Ax^3 + Bx^2 + Cx + D)\cos(4x) + (Ex^3 + Fx^2 + Gx + H)\sin(4x) </md>
					Separate guesses for <m>x^3\sin(4x)</m> and <m>x^2\sin(4x)</m> would give the <m>x^2</m>, <m>x</m>, and constant terms two letters each. The single cubic form already contains them.
					The terms in <m>y_h</m> carry the factor <m>e^{x/14}</m> and have frequency <m>\frac{\sqrt{55}}{14}</m>, not <m>4</m>, so none of the terms in this guess appear in <m>y_h</m> and the guess stands.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercisegroup>

	<exercisegroup cols="2">
		<title>🏋️‍♂️ Particular Solution</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				In these exercises, go one step beyond identifying the <em>form</em> of <m>y_p</m> and actually solve for the coefficients and write down a particular solution.
			</p>

			<p>
				Remember:
				<ul>
					<li>Find <m>y_h</m> first, to identify duplicates.</li>
					<li>Guess <m>y_p</m> based on the forcing term.</li>
					<li>Modify <m>y_p</m> if there's overlap with <m>y_h</m>.</li>
					<li>Plug into the DE, solve for the coefficients, and write the final <m>y_p</m>.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<!-- 1. Basic exponential forcing (easiest) -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-20">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = 3e^{5t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y_p = -\frac{3}{7} e^{5t}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow> r^2 - 4r - 12 \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> (r - 6)(r + 2) \amp = 0 </mrow>
						<mrow> r \amp = -2, 6 </mrow>
					</md>
					Thus, <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-2t} + c_2 e^{6t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					With a forcing term of <m>3e^{5t}</m>, we guess <m>y_p = A e^{5t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Substitute <m>y_p</m> into the DE:
					<md>
						<mrow> 25A e^{5t} - 4(5A e^{5t}) - 12(A e^{5t}) \amp = 3 e^{5t} </mrow>
						<mrow> -7A e^{5t} \amp = 3 e^{5t} </mrow>
					</md>
					Matching coefficients gives <m>A = -\frac{3}{7}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Therefore:
					<md>y_p = -\frac{3}{7} e^{5t}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 2. Simple trig forcing -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-21">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = \sin(2t)</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y_p = \frac{1}{40} \cos(2t) - \frac{1}{20} \sin(2t)</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The homogeneous equation is the same as above: 
					<m>y_h = c_1 e^{-2t} + c_2 e^{6t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Since the forcing term is <m>\sin(2t)</m>, try <m>y_p = A \cos(2t) + B \sin(2t)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Differentiate and substitute into the DE, collecting coefficients of <m>\sin(2t)</m> and <m>\cos(2t)</m>. Solve for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. 
					The system yields:
					<md>A = \frac{1}{40}, \quad B = -\frac{1}{20}</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Thus:
					<md>y_p = \frac{1}{40} \cos(2t) - \frac{1}{20} \sin(2t)</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 3. Cubic polynomial forcing -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-22">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = 2t^3 - t + 3</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y_p = -\frac{1}{6} t^3 + \frac{1}{6} t^2 - \frac{1}{9} t - \frac{5}{27}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Again, <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-2t} + c_2 e^{6t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The forcing term is a cubic polynomial. Guess:
					<md>y_p = A t^3 + B t^2 + C t + D</md>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Differentiate, substitute into the DE, and match coefficients.  
					Solving yields:
					<md>A = -\frac{1}{6}, \; B = \frac{1}{6}, \; C = -\frac{1}{9}, \; D = -\frac{5}{27}</md>.
				</p>
				<p>
					So:
					<md>y_p = -\frac{1}{6} t^3 + \frac{1}{6} t^2 - \frac{1}{9} t - \frac{5}{27}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<!-- 4. Overlap with homogeneous solution (requires t adjustment) -->
		<exercise label="uc-exercises-ex-23">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = e^{6t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y_p = \frac{1}{8} t e^{6t}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The homogeneous solution is <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-2t} + c_2 e^{6t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					A naive guess would be <m>y_p = A e^{6t}</m>, but <m>e^{6t}</m> already appears in <m>y_h</m>.
					</p>
				<p>
					Modify guess to <m>y_p = At e^{6t}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Substitute into the DE, solve for <m>A</m>, and find <m>A = \frac{1}{8}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Therefore:
					<md>y_p = \frac{1}{8} t e^{6t}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercisegroup>

	<exercisegroup cols="2">
		<title>✍🏻 General Solutions - Method of Undetermined Coefficients</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find the general solution to each differential equation using the five-step Method of Undetermined Coefficients:
				<ol>
					<li>Find the homogeneous solution <m>y_h</m>.</li>
					<li>Guess a form for <m>y_p</m> based on the forcing function.</li>
					<li>Modify <m>y_p</m> if it overlaps with <m>y_h</m>.</li>
					<li>Substitute <m>y_p</m> into the DE and solve for coefficients.</li>
					<li>Write the general solution <m>y = y_h + y_p</m>.</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-1">
			<statement><p><m>2x' + x = 3t^2</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>x(t) = c_1 e^{-\frac{1}{2} t} + 3t^2 - 12t + 24</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>2x' + x = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>2r + 1 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = -\frac{1}{2}</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>x_h = c_1 e^{-\frac{1}{2} t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function <m>3t^2</m> is a polynomial of degree 2.  
					Guess <m>x_p = At^2 + Bt + C</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap with <m>x_h</m>, so no modification needed.</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute <m>x_p</m> into the DE:
					<md>
						<mrow>x_p' \amp = 2At + B</mrow>
						<mrow>2(2At + B) + (At^2 + Bt + C) \amp = 3t^2</mrow>
						<mrow>At^2 + (4A+B)t + (2B+C) \amp = 3t^2</mrow>
					</md>
					Matching coefficients:  
					<m>A = 3</m>, <m>B = -12</m>, <m>C = 24</m>.  
					So <m>x_p = 3t^2 - 12t + 24</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> Write the general solution:
					<md>x(t) = c_1 e^{-\frac{1}{2} t} + 3t^2 - 12t + 24</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-2">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 5x + 1</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y(x) = c_1 e^{x} + c_2 e^{2x} + \frac{5}{2}x + \frac{17}{4}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>r^2 - 3r + 2 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>(r-1)(r-2) \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = 1,\,2</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{x} + c_2 e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function <m>5x + 1</m> is a polynomial of degree 1.
					Guess <m>y_p = Ax + B</m>.
				</p>

				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap with <m>y_h</m>, so no modification needed.</p>

				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute <m>y_p</m> into the DE:
					<md>
						<mrow>y_p' \amp = A, \quad y_p'' = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>0 - 3A + 2(Ax + B) \amp = 5x + 1</mrow>
						<mrow>2Ax + (2B - 3A) \amp = 5x + 1</mrow>
					</md>
					Matching coefficients:
					<m>2A = 5 \implies A = \frac{5}{2}</m> and
					<m>2B - 3A = 1 \implies 2B = 1 + \frac{15}{2} \implies B = \frac{17}{4}</m>.
					So <m>y_p = \frac{5}{2}x + \frac{17}{4}</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> Write the general solution:
					<md>y(x) = c_1 e^{x} + c_2 e^{2x} + \frac{5}{2}x + \frac{17}{4}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-3">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 5y = t + 2e^{-t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 e^{5t} + c_2 e^{-t} - \frac{1}{5}t + \frac{4}{25} - \frac{1}{3} t e^{-t}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 4y' - 5y = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>r^2 - 4r - 5 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>(r-5)(r+1) \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = 5,\,-1</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{5t} + c_2 e^{-t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function is <m>t + 2e^{-t}</m>.  
					Guess <m>y_p = (At + B) + C e^{-t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 3.</term> Because <m>e^{-t}</m> appears in <m>y_h</m>, multiply the form <m>C e^{-t}</m> by <m>t</m>; the polynomial form <m>At + B</m> shares no term with <m>y_h</m> and is left alone:
					<m>y_p = (At + B) + C t e^{-t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE:
					<md>
						<mrow>y_p' = A + (-Ct + C) e^{-t}</mrow>
						<mrow>y_p'' = (Ct - 2C) e^{-t}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Plugging in and matching coefficients gives:  
					<m>A = -\frac{1}{5}</m>, <m>B = \frac{4}{25}</m>, <m>C = -\frac{1}{3}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> Write the general solution:
					<md>y(t) = c_1 e^{5t} + c_2 e^{-t} - \frac{1}{5}t + \frac{4}{25} - \frac{1}{3} t e^{-t}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-4">
			<statement><p><m>z'' - 6z' + 34z = 650 \sin(6x)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>z(x) = c_1 e^{3x} \cos 5x + c_2 e^{3x} \sin 5x + 18 \cos 6x - \sin 6x</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>z'' - 6z' + 34z = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>r^2 - 6r + 34 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = 3 \pm 5i</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>z_h = c_1 e^{3x} \cos 5x + c_2 e^{3x} \sin 5x</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function is <m>650 \sin(6x)</m>.  
					Guess <m>z_p = A \cos 6x + B \sin 6x</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap with <m>z_h</m>, so no modification needed.</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE:
					<md>
						<mrow>z_p' = -6A \sin 6x + 6B \cos 6x</mrow>
						<mrow>z_p'' = -36A \cos 6x - 36B \sin 6x</mrow>
					</md>
					Matching coefficients from the DE gives <m>A = 18</m> and <m>B = -1</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> Write the general solution:
					<md>z(x) = c_1 e^{3x} \cos 5x + c_2 e^{3x} \sin 5x + 18 \cos 6x - \sin 6x</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-5">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' + 4y = 8\cos x + 12 e^{2x}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(x) = c_1 e^{2x} + c_2 x e^{2x} + \frac{24}{25}\cos x - \frac{32}{25}\sin x + 6x^2 e^{2x}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 4y' + 4y = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>r^2 - 4r + 4 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>(r-2)^2 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = 2 \text{ (double root)}</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{2x} + c_2 x e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function is <m>8\cos x + 12 e^{2x}</m>.  
					Guess <m>y_p = (A \cos x + B \sin x) + C e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 3.</term> Because <m>e^{2x}</m> appears in <m>y_h</m>, multiply the form <m>C e^{2x}</m> (the part of <m>y_p</m> matching <m>12 e^{2x}</m>) by <m>x</m>, then check again; the pair <m>A \cos x + B \sin x</m> shares no term with <m>y_h</m> and is left alone:
					<m>y_p = (A \cos x + B \sin x) + C x e^{2x}</m>.
					Still overlap (the term <m>x e^{2x}</m> is also in <m>y_h</m>), so multiply that form by another <m>x</m>:
					<m>y_p = (A \cos x + B \sin x) + C x^2 e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE, collect terms, and match coefficients:
					<md>
						<mrow>3A - 4B = 8</mrow>
						<mrow>4A + 3B = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>2C = 12</mrow>
					</md>
					Solve: <m>C = 6</m>, <m>A = \frac{24}{25}</m>, <m>B = -\frac{32}{25}</m>.  
					So <m>y_p = \frac{24}{25}\cos x - \frac{32}{25}\sin x + 6x^2 e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> Write the general solution:
					<md>y(x) = c_1 e^{2x} + c_2 x e^{2x} + \frac{24}{25}\cos x - \frac{32}{25}\sin x + 6x^2 e^{2x}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-6">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - y' - 6y = 2t + 3e^{3t} - e^{-2t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{3}t + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{3}{5}t e^{3t} + \frac{1}{5} t e^{-2t}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - y' - 6y = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>r^2 - r - 6 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>(r-3)(r+2) \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = 3,\,-2</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-2t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function is <m>2t + 3e^{3t} - e^{-2t}</m>.  
					Guess <m>y_p = (At + B) + C e^{3t} + D e^{-2t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 3.</term> Both <m>e^{3t}</m> and <m>e^{-2t}</m> appear in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply those terms by <m>t</m>:  
					<m>y_p = (At + B) + Ct e^{3t} + Dt e^{-2t}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE, collect terms, and match coefficients:
					<md>
						<mrow>-6A = 2 \implies A = -\frac{1}{3}</mrow>
						<mrow>-A - 6B = 0 \implies B = \frac{1}{18}</mrow>
						<mrow>5C = 3 \implies C = \frac{3}{5}</mrow>
						<mrow>-5D = -1 \implies D = \frac{1}{5}</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_p = -\frac{1}{3}t + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{3}{5}t e^{3t} + \frac{1}{5} t e^{-2t}</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> Write the general solution:
					<md>y(t) = c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{3}t + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{3}{5}t e^{3t} + \frac{1}{5} t e^{-2t}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-7">
			<statement><p><m>y'' + 3y' + 2y = 6e^{2x}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(x) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{-2x} + \frac{1}{2} e^{2x}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' + 3y' + 2y = 0</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>r^2 + 3r + 2 \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>(r+1)(r+2) \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>r \amp = -1,\,-2</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{-2x}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing function is <m>6e^{2x}</m>.  
					Guess <m>y_p = A e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>
	
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap with <m>y_h</m>, so no modification is needed.</p>
	
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute <m>y_p</m> into the DE:
					<md>
						<mrow>y_p' \amp = 2A e^{2x}, \ y_p'' = 4A e^{2x}</mrow>
						<mrow>4A e^{2x} + 3(2A e^{2x}) + 2(A e^{2x}) \amp = 6 e^{2x}</mrow>
						<mrow>12A e^{2x} \amp = 6 e^{2x}</mrow>
						<mrow>A \amp = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>y_p = \frac{1}{2} e^{2x}</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
					<md>y(x) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{-2x} + \frac{1}{2} e^{2x}</md>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-8">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 3y' + y = 2x^2 + 3x</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(x) = c_1 e^{\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2} x} + c_2 e^{\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}{2} x} + 2x^2 + 15x + 41</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 3y' + y = 0</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>r^2 - 3r + 1 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = \frac{3 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2} x} + c_2 e^{\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}{2} x}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing function is a degree‑2 polynomial.  
				Guess <m>y_p = Ax^2 + Bx + C</m>.
			</p>

			<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap with <m>y_h</m>, so no modification needed.</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE and match coefficients:
				<md>
					<mrow>y_p' \amp = 2Ax + B, \ y_p'' = 2A</mrow>
					<mrow>2A - 3(2Ax + B) + (Ax^2 + Bx + C) \amp = 2x^2 + 3x</mrow>
					<mrow>Ax^2 + (-6A + B)x + (2A - 3B + C) \amp = 2x^2 + 3x</mrow>
				</md>
				Compare coefficients:  
				<m>A = 2</m>, <m>-6A + B = 3 \Rightarrow B = 15</m>,  
				<m>2A - 3B + C = 0 \Rightarrow C = 41</m>.  
				So <m>y_p = 2x^2 + 15x + 41</m>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(x) = c_1 e^{\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2} x} + c_2 e^{\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}{2} x} + 2x^2 + 15x + 41</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-9">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = t e^{4t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 e^{6t} + c_2 e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{12} t e^{4t} - \frac{1}{36} e^{4t}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = 0</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>r^2 - 4r - 12 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = 6,\ -2</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{6t} + c_2 e^{-2t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing function is <m>t e^{4t}</m>.  
				Guess <m>y_p = (At + B) e^{4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap with <m>y_h</m> since <m>e^{4t}</m> is not in <m>y_h</m>.</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE:
				<md>
					<mrow>y_p' \amp = A e^{4t} + (4At + 4B) e^{4t}</mrow>
					<mrow>y_p'' \amp = 4A e^{4t} + (4A + 16B + 16At) e^{4t}</mrow>
					<mrow>y_p'' - 4y_p' - 12y_p \amp = (-12At + 4A - 12B) e^{4t} = t e^{4t}</mrow>
				</md>
				Matching coefficients gives <m>-12A = 1</m> and <m>4A - 12B = 0</m>, so
				<m>A = -\frac{1}{12}</m>, <m>B = -\frac{1}{36}</m>.
				So <m>y_p = -\frac{1}{12} t e^{4t} - \frac{1}{36} e^{4t}</m>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(t) = c_1 e^{6t} + c_2 e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{12} t e^{4t} - \frac{1}{36} e^{4t}</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-10">
			<statement><p><m>y'' + y = 3 \cos x</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(x) = c_1 \cos x + c_2 \sin x + \frac{3}{2} x \sin x</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' + y = 0</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>r^2 + 1 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = \pm i</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 \cos x + c_2 \sin x</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing function is <m>3 \cos x</m>.  
				Guess <m>y_p = A \cos x + B \sin x</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 3.</term> Both <m>\cos x</m> and <m>\sin x</m> appear in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply guess by <m>x</m>:  
				<m>y_p = x(A \cos x + B \sin x)</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE and match coefficients to find:  
				<m>A = 0</m>, <m>B = \frac{3}{2}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(x) = c_1 \cos x + c_2 \sin x + \frac{3}{2} x \sin x</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-11">
			<statement><p><m>y'' + 3y' - 28y = 7t + e^{4t} - 1</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 e^{-7t} + c_2 e^{4t} - \frac{1}{4}t + \frac{1}{112} + \frac{1}{11} t e^{4t}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' + 3y' - 28y = 0</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>r^2 + 3r - 28 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>(r+7)(r-4) \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = -7,\ 4</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-7t} + c_2 e^{4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing function is <m>7t + e^{4t} - 1</m>.  
				Guess <m>y_p = (At + B) + C e^{4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 3.</term> Because <m>e^{4t}</m> appears in <m>y_h</m>, multiply that term by <m>t</m>:  
				<m>y_p = (At + B) + C t e^{4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE, collect terms, and solve for coefficients:  
				<m>A = -\frac{1}{4}</m>, <m>B = \frac{1}{112}</m>, <m>C = \frac{1}{11}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(t) = c_1 e^{-7t} + c_2 e^{4t} - \frac{1}{4}t + \frac{1}{112} + \frac{1}{11} t e^{4t}</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-12">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 100y = 9 t^2 e^{10t} + \cos t - t \sin t</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 e^{10t} + c_2 e^{-10t} + \left(\frac{3}{20} t^3 - \frac{9}{400} t^2 + \frac{9}{4000} t\right) e^{10t} - \frac{99}{10201} \cos t + \frac{1}{101} t \sin t</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Solve the homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 100y = 0</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>r^2 - 100 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = \pm 10</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{10t} + c_2 e^{-10t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing function combines two forms:
				<ul>
					<li><m>9 t^2 e^{10t}</m> (poly <times/> exponential): guess <m>(A t^2 + B t + C) e^{10t}</m></li>
					<li><m>\cos t - t \sin t</m> (degree-1 poly <times/> trig, frequency 1): guess <m>(Dt + E)\cos t + (Ft + G)\sin t</m></li>
				</ul>
				The terms <m>\cos t</m> and <m>- t \sin t</m> share one sine-cosine form, so we do not guess them separately. Separate guesses for <m>\cos t</m> and for <m>- t \sin t</m> would each bring their own <m>\cos t</m> and <m>\sin t</m> terms, so <m>\cos t</m> and <m>\sin t</m> would each carry two coefficients, and those coefficients could not be pinned down uniquely. The starting guess is
				<md>y_p = (A t^2 + B t + C) e^{10t} + (Dt + E)\cos t + (Ft + G)\sin t</md>
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 3.</term> The term <m>C e^{10t}</m> also appears in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply every term of that form by <m>t</m>. No term of the trig form appears in <m>y_h</m>, so it stays as is:
				<md>y_p = (A t^3 + B t^2 + C t) e^{10t} + (Dt + E)\cos t + (Ft + G)\sin t</md>
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute into the DE and match coefficients for each independent function:
				<ul>
					<li><m>t^2 e^{10t}</m>, <m>t e^{10t}</m>, <m>e^{10t}</m>: <m>60A = 9</m>, <m>6A + 40B = 0</m>, <m>2B + 20C = 0</m>, so <m>A = \frac{3}{20}</m>, <m>B = -\frac{9}{400}</m>, <m>C = \frac{9}{4000}</m>.</li>
					<li><m>t \cos t</m>, <m>t \sin t</m>, <m>\cos t</m>, <m>\sin t</m>: <m>-101D = 0</m>, <m>-101F = -1</m>, <m>2F - 101E = 1</m>, <m>-2D - 101G = 0</m>, so <m>D = 0</m>, <m>F = \frac{1}{101}</m>, <m>E = -\frac{99}{10201}</m>, <m>G = 0</m>.</li>
				</ul>
				So <m>y_p = \left(\frac{3}{20} t^3 - \frac{9}{400} t^2 + \frac{9}{4000} t\right) e^{10t} - \frac{99}{10201} \cos t + \frac{1}{101} t \sin t</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(t) = c_1 e^{10t} + c_2 e^{-10t} + \left(\frac{3}{20} t^3 - \frac{9}{400} t^2 + \frac{9}{4000} t\right) e^{10t} - \frac{99}{10201} \cos t + \frac{1}{101} t \sin t</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-13">
			<statement><p><m>4y'' + y = e^{-2t} \sin \left( \frac{t}{2} \right) + 6 t \cos \left( \frac{t}{2} \right)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{1}{40} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \frac{1}{20} \sin \frac{t}{2}\right) e^{-2t} + \frac{3}{2} t \cos \frac{t}{2} + \frac{3}{4} t^2 \sin \frac{t}{2}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Homogeneous equation: <m>4y'' + y = 0</m>.
				<md>
					<mrow>4r^2 + 1 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = \pm \frac{i}{2}</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing has two pieces, each with its own form:
				<ul>
					<li><m>e^{-2t} \sin \left( \frac{t}{2} \right)</m> → guess <m>(A \cos \frac{t}{2} + B \sin \frac{t}{2}) e^{-2t}</m></li>
					<li><m>6 t \cos \left( \frac{t}{2} \right)</m> → guess <m>(C t + D) \cos \frac{t}{2} + (E t + F) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m></li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 3.</term> The terms <m>D \cos \frac{t}{2}</m> and <m>F \sin \frac{t}{2}</m> of the second form appear in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply every term of that form by <m>t</m>. Its new terms (<m>t \cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>t \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>t^2 \cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>t^2 \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>) are not in <m>y_h</m>, so one factor of <m>t</m> is enough. The first form carries the factor <m>e^{-2t}</m>, so none of its terms appear in <m>y_h</m> and it stays as is:
				<md>y_p = (A \cos \frac{t}{2} + B \sin \frac{t}{2}) e^{-2t} + (C t^2 + D t) \cos \frac{t}{2} + (E t^2 + F t) \sin \frac{t}{2}</md>
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute all into the DE and match coefficients for each independent function:
				<ul>
					<li><m>e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>e^{-2t} \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>: <m>16A - 8B = 0</m>, <m>8A + 16B = 1</m>, so <m>A = \frac{1}{40}</m>, <m>B = \frac{1}{20}</m>.</li>
					<li><m>t \cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>t \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>\cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>\sin \frac{t}{2}</m>: <m>8E = 6</m>, <m>-8C = 0</m>, <m>8C + 4F = 0</m>, <m>8E - 4D = 0</m>, so <m>C = 0</m>, <m>D = \frac{3}{2}</m>, <m>E = \frac{3}{4}</m>, <m>F = 0</m>.</li>
				</ul>
				So <m>y_p = \left(\frac{1}{40} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \frac{1}{20} \sin \frac{t}{2}\right) e^{-2t} + \frac{3}{2} t \cos \frac{t}{2} + \frac{3}{4} t^2 \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(t) = c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{1}{40} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \frac{1}{20} \sin \frac{t}{2}\right) e^{-2t} + \frac{3}{2} t \cos \frac{t}{2} + \frac{3}{4} t^2 \sin \frac{t}{2}</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-14">
			<statement><p><m>4y'' + 16y' + 17y = e^{-2t} \sin \left( \frac{t}{2} \right) + 6 t \cos \left( \frac{t}{2} \right)</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = e^{-2t}\big(c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2}\big) - \frac{1}{4} t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{10} t - \frac{6}{25}\right) \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{20} t - \frac{39}{200}\right) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Homogeneous equation: <m>4y'' + 16y' + 17y = 0</m>.
				<md>
					<mrow>4r^2 + 16r + 17 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = -2 \pm \frac{i}{2}</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = e^{-2t}\big(c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2}\big)</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> The forcing has the same two pieces as the previous exercise, but <m>y_h</m> is different here, so the overlap check comes out differently. Starting forms:
				<ul>
					<li><m>e^{-2t} \sin \left( \frac{t}{2} \right)</m> → guess <m>(A \cos \frac{t}{2} + B \sin \frac{t}{2}) e^{-2t}</m></li>
					<li><m>6 t \cos \left( \frac{t}{2} \right)</m> → guess <m>(C t + D) \cos \frac{t}{2} + (E t + F) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m></li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 3.</term> Both terms of the first form, <m>e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2}</m> and <m>e^{-2t} \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>, appear in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply that whole form by <m>t</m>. Its new terms <m>t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2}</m> and <m>t e^{-2t} \sin \frac{t}{2}</m> are not in <m>y_h</m>, so one factor of <m>t</m> is enough. The second form has no <m>e^{-2t}</m> factor, so none of its terms is in <m>y_h</m> and it stays as is:
				<md>
					<mrow>y_{p1} \amp = t (A \cos \frac{t}{2} + B \sin \frac{t}{2}) e^{-2t}</mrow>
					<mrow>y_{p2} \amp = (C t + D) \cos \frac{t}{2} + (E t + F) \sin \frac{t}{2}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute and match coefficients for all six unknowns:
				<ul>
					<li><m>e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>e^{-2t} \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>: <m>4B = 0</m>, <m>-4A = 1</m>, so <m>A = -\frac{1}{4}</m>, <m>B = 0</m>.</li>
					<li><m>t \cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>t \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>: <m>16C + 8E = 6</m>, <m>-8C + 16E = 0</m>, so <m>C = \frac{3}{10}</m>, <m>E = \frac{3}{20}</m>.</li>
					<li><m>\cos \frac{t}{2}</m>, <m>\sin \frac{t}{2}</m>: <m>16C + 16D + 4E + 8F = 0</m>, <m>-4C - 8D + 16E + 16F = 0</m>, so <m>D = -\frac{6}{25}</m>, <m>F = -\frac{39}{200}</m>.</li>
				</ul>
				So <m>y_p = -\frac{1}{4} t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{10} t - \frac{6}{25}\right) \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{20} t - \frac{39}{200}\right) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(t) = e^{-2t}\left(c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2}\right) - \frac{1}{4} t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{10} t - \frac{6}{25}\right) \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{20} t - \frac{39}{200}\right) \sin \frac{t}{2}</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="uc-gen-15">
			<statement><p><m>y'' + 8y' + 16y = e^{-4t} + (t^2 + 5)e^{-4t}</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p><m>y(t) = c_1 e^{-4t} + c_2 t e^{-4t} + \left(\frac{1}{12} t^4 + 3 t^2\right) e^{-4t}</m></p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
			<p>
				<term>Step 1.</term> Solve homogeneous equation <m>y'' + 8y' + 16y = 0</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>r^2 + 8r + 16 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>(r+4)^2 \amp = 0</mrow>
					<mrow>r \amp = -4 \text{ (double root)}</mrow>
				</md>
				So <m>y_h = c_1 e^{-4t} + c_2 t e^{-4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing function is <m>e^{-4t} + (t^2 + 5) e^{-4t} = (t^2 + 6) e^{-4t}</m>, a single polynomial <times/> exponential form.
				Guess <m>y_p = (A t^2 + B t + C) e^{-4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 3.</term> The terms <m>C e^{-4t}</m> and <m>B t e^{-4t}</m> appear in <m>y_h</m>. Multiplying the form by <m>t</m> gives <m>(A t^3 + B t^2 + C t) e^{-4t}</m>, which still shares <m>C t e^{-4t}</m> with <m>y_h</m>, so multiply by <m>t</m> again:
				<m>y_p = (A t^4 + B t^3 + C t^2) e^{-4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute, match coefficients and solve for <m>A,B,C</m>:
				<md>
					<mrow>y_p'' + 8y_p' + 16y_p \amp = (12A t^2 + 6B t + 2C) e^{-4t} = (t^2 + 6) e^{-4t}</mrow>
				</md>
				so <m>A = \frac{1}{12}</m>, <m>B = 0</m>, <m>C = 3</m>, and <m>y_p = \left(\frac{1}{12} t^4 + 3 t^2\right) e^{-4t}</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				<term>Step 5.</term> General solution:
				<md>y(t) = c_1 e^{-4t} + c_2 t e^{-4t} + \left(\frac{1}{12} t^4 + 3 t^2\right) e^{-4t}</md>
			</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercisegroup>

	<exercisegroup>
		<title>✍🏻 Initial Value Problems - Easy to Moderate</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				These IVPs involve either first-order linear DEs or simpler second-order equations with single forcing terms. Follow the UC workflow:
				<ol>
					<li>Find <m>y_h</m> (the homogeneous solution).</li>
					<li>Guess <m>y_p</m> (the particular solution).</li>
					<li>Adjust <m>y_p</m> if it overlaps with <m>y_h</m>.</li>
					<li>Substitute <m>y_p</m> and solve for coefficients.</li>
					<li>Write <m>y = y_h + y_p</m>.</li>
					<li>Apply initial condition(s) to find the specific solution.</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise label="c8-ivp-1">
			<statement><p><m>y' - 3y = 6,\quad y(0) = 5</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y(x) = 7e^{3x} - 2</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p><term>Step 1.</term> Homogeneous: <m>y_h = C e^{3x}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 2.</term> Forcing is constant 6 → guess <m>y_p = A</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap.</p>
				<p><term>Step 4.</term> Substitute: <m>-3A = 6 → A = -2</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 5.</term> General: <m>y = C e^{3x} - 2</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 6.</term> Use <m>y(0)=5</m>: <m>C = 7</m>. <md>y = 7e^{3x} - 2</md>.</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="c8-ivp-2">
			<statement><p><m>2x' + x = 3t^2,\quad x(0) = 15</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>x(t) = -9e^{-t/2} + 3t^2 -12t + 24</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p><term>Step 1.</term> Homog: <m>2x' + x=0 → x_h = C e^{-t/2}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 2.</term> Guess <m>x_p = At^2 + Bt + C_0</m> (poly deg 2).</p>
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap.</p>
				<p><term>Step 4.</term> Sub in: match coefficients → <m>A=3</m>, <m>B=-12</m>, <m>C_0=24</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 5.</term> General: <m>x = C e^{-t/2} + 3t^2 -12t + 24</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 6.</term> Use <m>x(0)=15</m>: <m>C=-9</m>.  <md>x(t) = -9e^{-t/2} + 3t^2 -12t + 24</md>.</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="c8-ivp-3">
			<statement><p><m>e^t z' = 1 - 4e^t z,\quad z(0) = \frac{4}{3}</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>z(t) = e^{-4t} + \frac{1}{3} e^{-t}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p><term>Step 1.</term> Rewrite: <m>z' + 4z = e^{-t}</m>, homog: <m>z_h = C e^{-4t}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 2.</term> Guess <m>z_p = A e^{-t}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap.</p>
				<p><term>Step 4.</term> Substitute: <m>3A e^{-t} = e^{-t} → A=\frac{1}{3}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 5.</term> General: <m>z = C e^{-4t} + \frac{1}{3} e^{-t}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 6.</term> Apply <m>z(0)=\frac{4}{3}</m>: <m>C=1</m>. <md>z(t) = e^{-4t} + \frac{1}{3} e^{-t}</md>.</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="c8-ivp-4">
			<statement><p><m>2z'' + z = 9e^{2t}, \ z(0)=3, \ z'(0)=-1</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>z(t) = 2\cos \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} - 3\sqrt{2} \sin \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} + e^{2t}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p><term>Step 1.</term> Homog: <m>2z'' + z=0 → r = \pm \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}</m>.  
					<m>z_h = c_1 \cos \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 2.</term> Guess <m>z_p = A e^{2t}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap.</p>
				<p><term>Step 4.</term> Substitute: <m>9A e^{2t} = 9 e^{2t} → A=1</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 5.</term> General: <md>z = c_1 \cos \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} + e^{2t}</md>.</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 6.</term> Apply ICs:  
					<md>
						<mrow>3 = c_1 + 1 → c_1 = 2</mrow>
						<mrow>-1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}c_2 + 2 → c_2 = -3\sqrt{2}</mrow>
					</md>
					So <m>z(t) = 2\cos \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} - 3\sqrt{2} \sin \frac{t}{\sqrt{2}} + e^{2t}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercisegroup>

	<exercisegroup>
		<title>✍🏻 Initial Value Problems - Advanced</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				These IVPs involve second-order DEs with multi-term forcing functions (polynomials, exponentials, trig). They require careful handling of overlaps and sometimes multiple adjustments to <m>y_p</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<exercise label="ivp-5">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - 4y' - 12y = 3 e^{5t}, \ y(0)=\frac{18}{7}, \ y'(0)=-\frac{1}{7}</m></p></statement>
			<answer><p><m>y(t) = e^{6t} + 2e^{-2t} - \frac{3}{7}e^{5t}</m></p></answer>
			<solution>
				<p><term>Step 1.</term> Homog: <m>y'' - 4y' - 12y=0 → r=6,-2 → y_h=c_1 e^{6t} + c_2 e^{-2t}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 2.</term> Guess <m>y_p = A e^{5t}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 3.</term> No overlap.</p>
				<p><term>Step 4.</term> Substitute: <m>-7A e^{5t} = 3e^{5t} → A=-\frac{3}{7}</m>.</p>
				<p><term>Step 5.</term> General: <m>y=c_1 e^{6t} + c_2 e^{-2t} - \frac{3}{7}e^{5t}</m>.</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 6.</term> Apply ICs:  
					<md>
						<mrow>c_1 + c_2 = 3</mrow>
						<mrow>6c_1 - 2c_2 = 2</mrow>
					</md>
					Solve → <m>c_1=1, c_2=2</m>.  
					<m>y(t) = e^{6t} + 2e^{-2t} - \frac{3}{7}e^{5t}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="ivp-6">
			<statement><p><m>y'' - y' - 6y = 2t + 3e^{3t} - e^{-2t},\ y(0)=0, \ y'(0)=0</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>y(t) = -\frac{26}{225} e^{3t} + \frac{3}{50} e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{3}t + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{3}{5}te^{3t} + \frac{1}{5}te^{-2t}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p><term>Step 1.</term> Homog: <m>r^2 - r - 6 = (r-3)(r+2) = 0</m>, so <m>r = 3, -2</m> and <m>y_h = c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-2t}</m>.</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing has three pieces:
					<ul>
						<li><m>2t</m> → guess <m>At + B</m></li>
						<li><m>3e^{3t}</m> → guess <m>C e^{3t}</m></li>
						<li><m>-e^{-2t}</m> → guess <m>D e^{-2t}</m></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 3.</term> <m>C e^{3t}</m> and <m>D e^{-2t}</m> each appear in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply each of those forms by <m>t</m>. The polynomial form <m>At + B</m> shares no term with <m>y_h</m> and is left alone:
					<md>y_p = At + B + C t e^{3t} + D t e^{-2t}</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute and match coefficients:
					<md>
						<mrow>t: \amp \quad -6A = 2 \implies A = -\frac{1}{3}</mrow>
						<mrow>1: \amp \quad -A - 6B = 0 \implies B = \frac{1}{18}</mrow>
						<mrow>e^{3t}: \amp \quad 5C = 3 \implies C = \frac{3}{5}</mrow>
						<mrow>e^{-2t}: \amp \quad -5D = -1 \implies D = \frac{1}{5}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p><term>Step 5.</term> General: <md>y = c_1 e^{3t} + c_2 e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{3}t + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{3}{5}te^{3t} + \frac{1}{5}te^{-2t}</md></p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 6.</term> Apply ICs:
					<md>
						<mrow>c_1 + c_2 + \frac{1}{18} \amp = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>3c_1 - 2c_2 - \frac{1}{3} + \frac{3}{5} + \frac{1}{5} \amp = 0</mrow>
					</md>
					Solve → <m>c_1 = -\frac{26}{225}, c_2 = \frac{3}{50}</m>.
					<m>y(t) = -\frac{26}{225} e^{3t} + \frac{3}{50} e^{-2t} - \frac{1}{3}t + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{3}{5}te^{3t} + \frac{1}{5}te^{-2t}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="ivp-7">
			<statement><p><m>4y'' + 16y' + 17y = e^{-2t} \sin \frac{t}{2} + 6 t \cos \frac{t}{2},\ y(0)=-1, \ y'(0)=0</m></p></statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>y(t) = e^{-2t}\big(-\frac{19}{25} \cos \frac{t}{2} - \frac{589}{200} \sin \frac{t}{2}\big) - \frac{1}{4} t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \big(\frac{3}{10} t - \frac{6}{25}\big) \cos \frac{t}{2} + \big(\frac{3}{20} t - \frac{39}{200}\big) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<term>Step 1.</term> Homog: <m>4r^2 + 16r + 17 = 0</m>, so <m>r = -2 \pm \frac{i}{2}</m> and <m>y_h = e^{-2t}\big(c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2}\big)</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 2.</term> Forcing has two pieces:
					<ul>
						<li><m>e^{-2t} \sin \frac{t}{2}</m> → guess <m>(A \cos \frac{t}{2} + B \sin \frac{t}{2}) e^{-2t}</m></li>
						<li><m>6 t \cos \frac{t}{2}</m> → guess <m>(C t + D) \cos \frac{t}{2} + (E t + F) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 3.</term> Both terms of the first form, <m>e^{-2t}\cos \frac{t}{2}</m> and <m>e^{-2t}\sin \frac{t}{2}</m>, appear in <m>y_h</m>, so multiply that whole form by <m>t</m>. The second form has no <m>e^{-2t}</m> factor, shares no term with <m>y_h</m>, and is left alone:
					<md>
						<mrow>y_{p1} \amp = t (A \cos \frac{t}{2} + B \sin \frac{t}{2}) e^{-2t}</mrow>
						<mrow>y_{p2} \amp = (C t + D) \cos \frac{t}{2} + (E t + F) \sin \frac{t}{2}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 4.</term> Substitute and match coefficients:
					<md>
						<mrow>e^{-2t}\sin \tfrac{t}{2}: \amp \quad -4A = 1</mrow>
						<mrow>e^{-2t}\cos \tfrac{t}{2}: \amp \quad 4B = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>t\cos \tfrac{t}{2}: \amp \quad 16C + 8E = 6</mrow>
						<mrow>t\sin \tfrac{t}{2}: \amp \quad -8C + 16E = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>\cos \tfrac{t}{2}: \amp \quad 16C + 16D + 4E + 8F = 0</mrow>
						<mrow>\sin \tfrac{t}{2}: \amp \quad -4C - 8D + 16E + 16F = 0</mrow>
					</md>
					so <m>A = -\frac{1}{4}</m>, <m>B = 0</m>, <m>C = \frac{3}{10}</m>, <m>D = -\frac{6}{25}</m>, <m>E = \frac{3}{20}</m>, <m>F = -\frac{39}{200}</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 5.</term> General:
					<md>y = e^{-2t}\left(c_1 \cos \frac{t}{2} + c_2 \sin \frac{t}{2}\right) - \frac{1}{4} t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{10} t - \frac{6}{25}\right) \cos \frac{t}{2} + \left(\frac{3}{20} t - \frac{39}{200}\right) \sin \frac{t}{2}</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					<term>Step 6.</term> Apply ICs:
					<md>
						<mrow>c_1 - \frac{6}{25} \amp = -1</mrow>
						<mrow>-2c_1 + \frac{1}{2}c_2 - \frac{1}{4} + \frac{3}{10} - \frac{39}{400} \amp = 0</mrow>
					</md>
					Solve → <m>c_1 = -\frac{19}{25}, c_2 = -\frac{589}{200}</m>.
					<m>y(t) = e^{-2t}\big(-\frac{19}{25} \cos \frac{t}{2} - \frac{589}{200} \sin \frac{t}{2}\big) - \frac{1}{4} t e^{-2t} \cos \frac{t}{2} + \big(\frac{3}{10} t - \frac{6}{25}\big) \cos \frac{t}{2} + \big(\frac{3}{20} t - \frac{39}{200}\big) \sin \frac{t}{2}</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercisegroup>
</exercises>
